<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harry Johnston</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Harry Johnston</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Harry Johnston was a British explorer and colonial administrator. As vice-consul in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-delta">Niger Delta</a> he deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaja-of-opobo">King Jaja</a> of Opobo in 1887. As commissioner of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> from 1891 he fought the Yao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> chiefs around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a>, founding Nyasaland, and as special commissioner in Uganda he negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buganda-agreement">Buganda Agreement</a> of 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-johnston.jpg" alt="Harry Johnston" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elliott &amp; Fry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Harry_Johnston.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Harry Hamilton Johnston was born on 12 June 1858 at Kennington Park in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. He studied at Stockwell grammar school and King's College London, and then painting for four years at the Royal Academy, travelling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, including the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. In 1882 he visited southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, and the following year he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> in the Congo. In 1884 the Royal Geographical Society and the British Association made him leader of a scientific expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro">Mount Kilimanjaro</a>, where he made treaties with local chiefs in competition with German efforts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In October 1886 Johnston was appointed vice-consul in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta, and in 1887, as acting consul, he deposed and banished the local chief Jaja. In 1888 he proposed a continuous band of British territory from the Cape to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> to Lord Salisbury, and he wrote articles in support.</p><p>In Nyasaland he arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blantyre">Blantyre</a> in March 1889, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> he proclaimed was endorsed by the Foreign Office in May 1891. He suppressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> of the Yao chiefs, one group at a time, until 1894. In 1895 his force bombarded and stormed the stockaded town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> leader Mlozi at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karonga">Karonga</a>; Mlozi was captured and hanged on 5 December; between 200 and 300 fighters and several hundred non-combatants were killed, many while trying to surrender.</p><p>Johnston also reviewed the land claims of Europeans, who had bought huge areas for trivial goods from chiefs with no understanding of English land tenure. He rejected very few and issued Certificates of Claim to the European claimants, which concentrated much of the most fertile land in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shire-highlands">Shire Highlands</a> in European hands for the rest of the colonial period. Until 1899 he also had administrative control of what became North-Eastern Rhodesia. In 1899 he went to Uganda as special commissioner and concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buganda-agreement">Buganda Agreement</a> of 1900, which divided the land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the chiefs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Johnston was made a Knight Commander of the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> in 1896 and a Knight Grand Cross of the Order of St Michael and St George in 1901. He stood for Parliament as a Liberal in 1903 and 1906 without success, and from 1906 lived at Poling in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sussex">West Sussex</a>, concentrating on writing; his autobiography, The Story of my Life, appeared in 1923. He suffered two strokes in 1925 and died on 31 July 1927. The okapi, Okapia johnstoni, carries his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harry Johnston” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the Buganda Agreement of 1900, negotiated by <b>Sir Harry Johnston</b> with the regents of the young Kabaka Daudi Chwa II, led by Katikkiro Apolo Kagwa, Britain recognised the kabaka, the Lukiiko and the chiefs, and distributed large estates of freehold mailo land to the chiefs and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>To block Portuguese claims, Britain declared the Shire Highlands a protectorate in 1889 and in 1891 proclaimed the British Central Africa Protectorate, under Commissioner <b>Harry Johnston</b> with his headquarters at Zomba.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-central-africa-protectorate">British Central Africa Protectorate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jumbe signed a treaty with Britain in 1889 and cooperated with <b>Harry Johnston</b> for a time, but in the mid-1890s the British removed the last Jumbe and absorbed Nkhotakota into the British Central Africa Protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/jumbe-state-of-nkhotakota">Jumbe State of Nkhotakota</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1887 the British consul <b>Harry Johnston</b> lured him aboard a ship and deported him; he died in 1891.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-opobo">Kingdom of Opobo</a></li><li><blockquote>Makanjira resisted the British Central Africa Protectorate under <b>Harry Johnston</b>; his towns were attacked from 1891, Fort Maguire was built on his land, and by 1895 he had been driven out and the chiefdom brought under colonial rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/makanjira-chiefdom">Makanjira Chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1907 the British Central Africa Protectorate, created by <b>Harry Johnston</b> in 1891 after wars against Yao slave traders, was renamed the Nyasaland Protectorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/nyasaland">Nyasaland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1902" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Harry Johnston"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1902">What was here in 1902? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
